{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793572406,"nonce":"Vqo9Ay/HtNe0zE3yL5nUb8vLWNc=","title":"HashLink's $asm opcode (OAsm) is ignored: lift its x86-64 bytes to AIR","message":"OAsm in HashLink\n\nHaxe source: untyped $asm(mode, value) or $asm(mode, value, reg), mode and value constant (genhl.ml). genhl emits OAsm(mode, value, reg+1); reg 0 means none. The only consumer is src/jit.c (x86-64 JIT): mode 0 writes the byte value into the code stream; mode 1 marks CPU register value scratch; mode 2 copies VM register reg into CPU register value; mode 3 copies CPU register value into VM register reg; mode 4 makes the function naked (must be the first opcode, no locals). hl2c emits 'UNSUPPORTED ASM OPCODE'. Nothing in the HashLink repo, Haxe std or Haxe's tests produces it.\n\nReal users found by code search: ncannasse/hlos (a bare-metal x86 kernel: port I/O 0xEC/0xEE, interrupts, naked functions) and hlbc's SpecialOpcodes test data. hlos is privileged code and out of scope for any lifter.\n\nAsh today\n\nDecodes and re-encodes OAsm (hl_op_OAsm, Opcode::Asm). The interpreter treats it as a no-op ('x86 specific'); BodyShape marks the function jit_unsupported so it stays interpreted; the optimiser treats it as ClobberAll. A function that relies on its asm silently computes something else.\n\nProposal (agreed in discussion): lift, do not emulate\n\nAt load, collect each run of mode-0 bytes between the mode 1-3 markers, decode with a pure-Rust x86-64 decoder (iced-x86 or similar, wasm-friendly), and translate to AIR on virtual registers, with modes 2/3 as the register in/out edges. Then every tier (interp, Cranelift, LLVM, AOT, wasm, and arm64 hosts) runs it as ordinary code and memory goes through AIR loads and stores, so the collector's rules hold. Subset: integer ALU, moves, shifts, compares and conditional jumps inside the fragment, scalar and packed SSE, with explicit flag modelling. Refuse at load, naming the instruction, anything else: privileged and I/O instructions, stack-pointer games, segment registers, syscall, self-modifying code, naked functions (mode 4).\n\nFirst step, small: make modes 0 and 4 fail loudly in the interpreter instead of silently no-oping, and print a clear message naming the function. Then collect real fragments before sizing the lifter: no program known to us uses a lifter-sized subset yet, so build it only when one appears.\n\nOpen: which program needs it; whether results must match HashLink's flag and SSE rounding bit for bit.","files":null}]}